    Vera Sans Mono, Copyright 2003 Bitstream Inc., licensed under the Bitstream
    Vera License with Reserved Font Names "Bitstream" and "Vera", and work from
    the DejaVu project committed to the public domain. See licenses/Hack.txt.
  Noto Emoji — Copyright the Noto Project Authors, SIL Open Font License 1.1.
    See licenses/OFL-1.1.txt.
  Ubuntu — Copyright 2010-2011 Canonical Ltd., Ubuntu Font Licence 1.0.
    See licenses/UFL-1.0.txt.
  emoji-icon-font — Copyright (c) 2014 John Slegers, MIT License.
    See licenses/emoji-icon-font-MIT.txt.

C libraries compiled from source
--------------------------------

  libgit2 — vendored and compiled in via git2's `vendored-libgit2` feature.
    Licensed GPL-2.0 with a linking exception that grants "unlimited permission
    to link the compiled version of this library into combinations with other
    programs, and to distribute those combinations without any restriction".
    Forge distributes only such a combination, never libgit2's source, so no
    copyleft obligation reaches Forge's own code. Named here because a licence
    scanner will report the GPL and the exception is the answer.
  OpenSSL 3.6.3 — vendored and compiled in via git2's `vendored-openssl`
    feature, licensed under the Apache License 2.0.
  libssh2 — compiled from source by `libssh2-sys`, reached through git2's `ssh`
    feature, licensed under the BSD 3-Clause License. The `libssh2-sys` crate
    itself is MIT or Apache-2.0; the C it builds is not.
  AWS-LC — compiled from C and per-architecture assembly by `aws-lc-sys`,
    reached through `russh`'s default `aws-lc-rs` cryptographic backend. Its
    terms are a conjunction: ISC, Apache-2.0, MIT, BSD-3-Clause, and
    third-party components offered under Apache-2.0, ISC or MIT-0. It also
    carries code derived from OpenSSL and BoringSSL.
  ring — compiles C and assembly for its cryptographic primitives (see the ISC
    entry below for its terms). Forge uses it directly for Ed25519 request
    signing.

Rust crates
-----------

Forge links a large number of Rust crates, declared in the Cargo.toml of each
workspace member and pinned in Cargo.lock, which together are the authoritative
dependency list. They are predominantly MIT and Apache-2.0. Worth naming
specifically, because their terms are not those two:

  Mozilla Public License 2.0 — option-ext, reached through `dirs`.
    MPL-2.0 section 3.3 permits distributing the larger work under other terms;
    section 3.2 asks that recipients of the executable be told how to obtain
    the source form of those files, which is: from crates.io at the versions
    pinned in Cargo.lock, or from each project's upstream repository.
  CDLA-Permissive-2.0 — webpki-roots, carrying Mozilla's CA certificate set.
  Unicode-3.0 — the ICU crates (icu_*, zerovec and related), whose data files
    carry Unicode, Inc.'s attribution terms.
  CC0-1.0 — notify, hexf-parse.
  ISC — untrusted, rustls-webpki, libloading. ISC is a permissive licence
    requiring attribution only; this entry is that attribution.
  Apache-2.0 AND ISC — ring, a direct dependency, used for Ed25519 request
    signing. The conjunction means both sets of terms apply, not a choice
    between them.
  ISC AND (Apache-2.0 OR ISC) — aws-lc-rs, reached through `russh`. Its
    `aws-lc-sys` companion additionally carries Apache-2.0, MIT,
    BSD-3-Clause and MIT-0 components; see the C libraries section above.
  BSD 3-Clause — curve25519-dalek, ed25519-dalek, subtle, reached through
    `russh`, and the vendored libssh2 C source. The clause prohibiting use of
    the authors' names for endorsement applies; Forge makes no such claim.
  Unicode-3.0 also applies to unicode-ident, in conjunction with its MIT or
    Apache-2.0 grant.


